media: h713-hdmirx: the source cap is what the firmware locks, not the panel

QUERY_DV_TIMINGS reports the SOURCE; G_FMT reports the ring (0136f). The cap
that says which sources may be reported was still the panel, 1920x1080 and
148.5 MHz, and that refused a source the rest of the chain handles.

Measured 21.09.2026 with a 3840x2160p30 source: the firmware locked it (its
own table row 126, signal id 0x0002008f, 4400x2250 at 30 Hz), INCAP read
active 3840x2160 and total 4400x2250, and the rowbyte still read 0x78 -
1920 bytes per luma line. That is the firmware scaling the source down inside
its capture path (T1 4b, VIncap_ConfigDownScaler), and 0136f's ring rule
turns it into exactly what it is: pitch 1920 is below ALIGN(3840, 16), so the
firmware scales, the ring is 1920x1080, and the picture scaler's window
confirms it to the pixel. Nothing downstream was in danger. Only the cap
refused, with -ERANGE, and h713-tv put the console up over a picture the
plane could have taken.

So the cap follows the table: 4096x2160 and 297 MHz are the largest enabled
records the firmware carries, and what the firmware can lock is what this
driver may report. The plane is protected by the ring rule, which is where
that protection belongs; the cap was never the thing that kept a 3840-wide
picture off a 1920-wide plane.

Interlaced stays refused. Since 0136h the reported scan type is the matched
table row's, so a source that lands on one of the fifteen interlaced records
- 1920x540i at 50 Hz is row 9, which a 1080i50 source matches when the
receiver reports fields - is refused on the row, not only on the firmware
record's own flag. How the capture lays fields out in the ring has still
never been looked at, and a timing this driver cannot describe is better
refused than described wrongly.

Follow-up to 0136j. Host model in umbau/work/T2/model: the 4K30 case now
reports 3840x2160p30 at 297 MHz with a 1920x1080 ring, and the interlaced
row is still refused.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

